    answer: `${fplain(perp(fr(1, 2)))}`,
    explain: `The tangent gradient is the negative reciprocal of the normal's: turn $\\frac{1}{2}$ over to get 2, then change the sign. Answering $\\frac{1}{2}$ or $-\\frac{1}{2}$ means one of the two moves was skipped.`,
  },

  // variant 4: an unknown coefficient
  { type: "h", text: "An unknown coefficient in the curve", role: "variant" },
  {
    type: "p",
    md: `Sometimes the curve carries a letter, and the gradient condition is what finds it. The tangent to ${M(`y = ax^{2} ${V4.b.n < 0 ? "-" : "+"} ${Math.abs(V4.b.n)}x`)} at ${M(`x = ${fplain(V4.x0)}`)} is parallel to ${M(lineLatex({ m: V4.lineM, c: V4.lineC }))}.\n${M(`\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2ax ${V4.b.n < 0 ? "-" : "+"} ${Math.abs(V4.b.n)}`)}, which is ${M(`${2 * V4.x0.n}a - ${Math.abs(V4.b.n)}`)} at ${M(`x = ${fplain(V4.x0)}`)}.\nParallel means equal gradients: ${M(`${2 * V4.x0.n}a - ${Math.abs(V4.b.n)} = ${fplain(V4.lineM)}`)}, so ${M(`a = ${fplain(V4_A)}`)}. Only then can the point and the tangent be found.`,
