  // going further: the hardest shape, two lines from two points and where they cross (Summer 2021 Q12)
  { type: "h", text: "Going further: where a tangent meets a normal", role: "further" },
  {
    type: "figure",
    alt: "The parabola y = x squared with the tangent at A, where x = 2, and the normal at B, where x = -1, drawn as dashed lines that cross at the point C.",
    svg: furtherSvg,
    caption: "The tangent at A and the normal at B are two different lines, and C is where they cross.",
  },
  {
    type: "p",
    md: `The hardest shape asks for two lines from two different points, then where they cross. On ${M("y = x^{2}")}, the tangent at ${M(`A${pointLatex(fr(2), TF.y0)}`)} is ${M(lineLatex(TF.line))} and the normal at ${M(`B${pointLatex(fr(-1), NF.y0)}`)} is ${M(lineLatex(NF.line))}.\nSetting them equal gives ${M(`${flatex(fsub(TF.line.m, NF.line.m))}x = ${flatex(fsub(NF.line.c, TF.line.c))}`)}, so ${M(`x = ${flatex(CF.x)}`)} exactly.`,
