    kind: "number",
    prompt: `The tangent $${lineLatex(G12.t)}$ and the normal $${lineLatex(G12.n)}$ are drawn on the same axes. At what value of $x$ do they cross?`,
    answer: fplain(G12_X),
    explain: `Setting them equal: $${lineLatex(G12.t).replace("y = ", "")} = ${lineLatex(G12.n).replace("y = ", "")}$, so $${flatex(fsub(G12.t.m, G12.n.m))}x = ${flatex(fsub(G12.n.c, G12.t.c))}$ and $x = ${fplain(G12_X)}$.`,
  },

  // derivation: why the normal's gradient is -1/m
  { type: "h", text: "Why perpendicular gradients multiply to −1", role: "derivation" },
  {
    type: "figure",
    alt: "A step of 1 across and m up drawn on the tangent, and the same step turned through a right angle on the normal, where it becomes m back and 1 up.",
    svg: derivSvg,
    caption: "Turn the tangent's step through a right angle and it becomes the normal's step.",
  },
  {
    type: "p",
    md: `Draw the tangent's gradient as a step: ${M("1")} across and ${M("m")} up. Turn that step through a right angle and it becomes ${M("m")} back and ${M("1")} up.\nSo the normal's gradient is ${M("\\frac{1}{-m} = -\\frac{1}{m}")}, and the two gradients multiply to ${M("m \\times -\\frac{1}{m} = -1")}. Every normal in this topic rests on that one turn.`,
  },
  {
    type: "gate",
    id: "g13",
    kind: "choice",
    prompt: `A step of $1$ across and $${fplain(G13_M)}$ up is turned through a right angle. What is the gradient of the turned step?`,
    options: [`$${flatex(G13)}$`, `$${flatex(fneg(G13_M))}$`, `$${flatex(finv(G13_M))}$`],
    answer: `$${flatex(G13)}$`,
    explain: `Turned, the step goes $${fplain(G13_M)}$ back and $1$ up, so its gradient is $\\frac{1}{-${fplain(G13_M)}} = ${flatex(G13)}$: the negative reciprocal of $${fplain(G13_M)}$.`,
  },

  {
    type: "callout",
    kind: "mustknow",
    title: "You can now",
    md: "Build a tangent: differentiate, substitute, take $y$ from the curve, write the line.\nBuild a normal the same way with $-\\frac{1}{m}$: turned over, sign changed.\nTurn a stated gradient, a parallel line or a normal's gradient into an equation for $\\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nFind an unknown coefficient, or where a tangent meets the curve again.\nSpot the twists: horizontal, two tangents compared, a power of $x$ underneath.",
  },
  { type: "h", text: "In the exam", role: "pointer" },
